<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quimper</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quimper is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finistere">Finistère</a>, western Brittany, at the confluence of the Odet and Steir rivers, and the historic capital of Cornouaille. It grew around the see traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-corentin">Saint Corentin</a>, whose Gothic cathedral dominates the town, and its counts became dukes of Brittany in the 11th century. It is known for its faience pottery and remains a centre of Breton culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quimper.jpg" alt="Quimper" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Romain Cormier · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quimper2011.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
